msgid "Keyring Setup on Linux Mint"
msgstr "Linux Mint'te anahtarlık kurulumu"

msgid ""
"Account passwords are kept in the system keyring (the Secret Service), never "
"on disk. The keyring did not accept the password, so this account will not "
"stay signed in after you close the app."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Account passwords are kept in the system keyring (the Secret Service), never "
"on disk. On Linux Mint with Cinnamon the keyring can need a one-time setup "
"to keep them, and to stop asking to be unlocked at every login."
msgstr ""

#. The command stays out of the translation: it is typed as is.
msgid ""
"Set it up:\n"
"1. Install the keyring tools if needed:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"2. Open Passwords and Keys (Seahorse). Make sure a keyring named “Login” "
"exists and is the default (right-click → Set as Default).\n"
"\n"
"To stop the unlock prompt at each login, choose one:\n"
"• Give the Login keyring the same password as your user account (right-click "
"→ Change Password) and log in with a password, not automatic login. The "
"keyring then unlocks when you log in. This is the safer choice.\n"
"• With automatic login, give the Login keyring an empty password. Your saved "
"passwords are then stored unencrypted, so do this only on a computer you "
"trust."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Run these steps on the host system, not inside the Flatpak: the app uses "
"your desktop’s keyring."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Make sure a Secret Service keyring is installed, running and unlocked. For "
"example, install gnome-keyring and seahorse (Passwords and Keys), create a "
"default “Login” keyring and give it your login password so it unlocks when "
"you log in."
msgstr ""

